# Implanting of Single Zinc Sites into 2D Metal–Organic Framework Nanozymes for Boosted Antibiofilm Therapy

## abstract
Design of nanozymes with catalytic active sites at atomic‐scale not only improves the atomic utilization, but also provides a well‐defined coordination structure for nanozymes' catalytic mechanism research. Herein, a surfactant‐assisted method is reported for preparing 2D metal–organic frameworks based single zinc sites nanozyme (SZN‐MOFs) through assembling preformed Zn single‐atom coordinated porphyrin precursors into ultrathin MOF nanosheets. The Zn atom loading weight ratio in SZN‐MOFs is up to 4.6 wt.%. The SZN‐MOFs exhibit extraordinary peroxidase‐like activity, which can effectively catalyze H2O2 into hydroxyl radicals. The catalytical mechanism is elucidated and the origin of the high peroxidase‐like activity of SZN‐MOFs is rationalized using density functional theory calculations. Finally, it is demonstrated that the SZN‐MOFs present great promises for in vitro and in vivo antibiofilm activity under a low concentration of H2O2. This study not only develops a surfactant‐assisted method for fabricating MOF‐based single sites nanozyme, but also manifests the applications in the field of antibiofilm therapy.

Design of nanozymes with catalytic active sites at atomic-scale not only improves the atomic utilization, but also provides a well-defined coordination structure for nanozymes’ catalytic mechanism research. Herein, a surfactantassisted method is reported for preparing 2D metal–organic frameworks based single zinc sites nanozyme (SZN-MOFs) through assembling preformed Zn single-atom coordinated porphyrin precursors into ultrathin MOF nanosheets. The Zn atom loading weight ratio in SZN-MOFs is up to $4.6~\mathrm{wt.}\%$ . The SZN-MOFs exhibit extraordinary peroxidase-like activity, which can effectively catalyze ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ into hydroxyl radicals. The catalytical mechanism is elucidated and the origin of the high peroxidase-like activity of SZNMOFs is rationalized using density functional theory calculations. Finally, it is demonstrated that the SZN-MOFs present great promises for in vitro and in vivo antibiofilm activity under a low concentration of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ . This study not only develops a surfactant-assisted method for fabricating MOF-based single sites nanozyme, but also manifests the applications in the field of antibiofilm therapy.
## 1. Introduction
Nanozymes, a class of nanomaterials with enzyme-like properties, have caused increasing research interest due to their unique advantages of low cost, high stability, and ease of storage.[1] Various natural enzymes, such as oxidoreductase, hydrolase, and ligase, have been successfully imitated by hundreds of nanomaterials.[1b,2] In addition, nanozymes exhibit promising applications in the field of biosensing, therapeutics, and environmental protection, etc.[3] Although significant progress having been made, there remain some challenges facing conventional nanozyme technologies.[1a] First, nanozymes suffer from low atom utilization efficiency. Second, the inhomogeneous elemental composition and diverse facet structure exposed by nanozymes result in unclear catalytic mechanism. To address these above issues of conventional nanozymes, single-site nanozymes (especially single-atom nanozymes), which integrate the characteristics of catalytic sites at atomic-scale and enzyme-like catalytic activities, were developed.[4] Single-site nanozymes could not only promote the atom economy for the metal usage, but provide new opportunities to elucidate nanozymes’ catalytic mechanism due to their well-defined coordination structure.[5]
site nanozymes have been demonstrated, including wet chemistry strategies, spatial confinement strategy, etc.[4d,6] However, fabricating single-site nanozymes with atomically isolated metal centers is challenging because the high surface free energy of single metal atoms significantly enhances their aggregation tendency. Therefore, to avoid the aggregation of thermodynamically driven unstable single metal atoms, the loading of single-site nanozymes from these methods was mostly $_{<2}$ wt. $\%$ .[7] The low metal loading of singlesite nanozymes would in turn limit their catalytic activity.[4d] On the other hand, numerous researches have demonstrated that single-atom metals supported on a nitrogen doped carbon framework with metal- ${\bf\cdot N_{4}}$ site possessed excellent enzyme-like activity.[4a,f,h] Some metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with a tetrahedral or square-planar coordination geometry can also provide a metal– ${\bf\cdot N_{4}}$ structure.[8] In addition, MOFs possess welldefined coordination network and adjustable porosity, which are promising for loading numerous single-atom metals.[9] Therefore, MOFs may act as ideal supports to directly fabricate the single-site nanozymes with high densities of active metals.[5c] However, the catalytic activity of bulk MOFs-based single-site nanozymes could be restricted due to large mass transfer resistance and limited accessibility of active sites. Since 2D MOFs with ultrathin thickness could impose fast kinetics and effectively lower the mass-transfer resistance of catalytic reactions, we envision that the 2D MOFs are the promising candidates for synthesizing highly active single-site nanozymes.[8,10]
Herein, the surfactant-assisted approach was conducted to the synthesis of 2D MOFs-based single zinc sites nanozyme (SZN-MOFs) by using Zinc (II) tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (Zn-TCPP) as the linker and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ clusters as the metal nodes (Scheme 1). Densely $Z\mathrm{n}$ single-atoms with a loading of 4.6  wt. $\%$ could be obtained in SZN-MOFs by introducing single zinc atoms into porphyrin rings before constructing 2D MOFs. The SZN-MOFs exhibited remarkable peroxidase (POD)-like activity due to the abundant accessible single-atom active sites and the small substrate diffusion resistance. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations provided a rationale for the POD-like reaction path of SZN-MOFs. To demonstrate the potential biomedical applications of SZN-MOFs, in vitro and in vivo antibiofilm therapies were performed (Scheme 1). Biofilm is a kind of multicellular community that formed by populations of pathogens adhered to substrates.[11] It is hard to eradicate biofilm infections efficiently using available antibacterial agents because the biofilm extracellular polymeric substances (EPS) not only serve as a guardian barrier against host immune defense but also decreases the penetration of antibacterial drugs.[12] Therefore, it is urgent to develop novel strategies for treating biofilm infection diseases.[13] The SZN-MOFs could efficiently catalyze the generation of toxic hydroxyl radicals (•OH) at a low concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(100~\upmu\mathbf{M})$ , which could effectively degrade EPS and deactivate intracellular organelles.[3e,12,14] In this study, SZN-MOFs exhibit significant antibiofilm activity with the assistance of a low dosage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , resulting in a biofilm survival rate of $<10\%$ in vitro. Furthermore, the in vivo biofilm infections experiments confirm that SZN-MOFs can efficiently enhance wound infection healing. Besides, SZN-MOFs have a negligible toxic adverse effect at the given concentrations.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Characterization of SZN-MOFs
First, Zn-TCPP was synthesized according to the previously reported method (Figures S1 and S2, Supporting Information).[15] Then, SZN-MOFs were prepared by the polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP) surfactant-assisted strategy.[8b] In the reaction, Zn-TCPP units were coordinated with $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ metal nodes, forming a $\mathrm{MOFs^{\prime}}$ structure. Meanwhile, the PVP molecules selectively attached on the $\mathrm{MOFs^{\prime}}$ surface to reduce the interlayer stacking, thus resulting in the anisotropic growth and then the formation of ultrathin nanosheets. The synthesized SZN-MOFs had a sheet-like morphology as confirmed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (Figures 1a,b). In comparison, similar methods without the assistance of PVP molecular could only obtain the bulk crystal of MOFs (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Atomic force microscopy (AFM) demonstrated that the average thickness of SZN-MOFs was ${\approx}2.5\pm0.6$ nm (Figure  1c; Figure S4, Supporting Information). Nitrogen adsorption–desorption assays showed that SZN-MOFs had a high specific surface area of $372{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ and a micropore size of ${\approx}1.3~\mathrm{nm}$ , which could significantly contribute to the contacting between substrate molecular and single zinc active sites in the catalytic reaction (Figure S5, Supporting Information). TEMelemental mapping and energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) suggested that C, N, O, Cu, and Zn elements were uniformly distributed in SZN-MOFs (Figure  1d). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) further confirmed the existence of these elements (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometer (ICP-AES) analysis demonstrated that the Zn weight ratio in SZN-MOFs was $4.6~\mathrm{wt}.\%$ , which was superior to most previously reported single-atom nanozymes (Table S1, Supporting Information). SZN-MOFs and bulk MOFs had similar powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) peaks, where no Zn nanoparticle diffraction peaks in SZN-MOFs were observed (Figure S7, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure  1e, no nanoparticles were observed in high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images. The atomically dispersed single zinc atoms on the SZN-MOFs were observed by high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images, revealed by large amounts of isolated bright dots (Figures 1f,g).
The high-resolution XPS and X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) were performed to investigate the atomic structures of SZN-MOFs. The high-resolution XPS spectrum showed that $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and $_\mathrm{C-OH}$ groups in SZN-MOFs were located at the positions of 531.3 and $532.9\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, which were consistent with those of Zn-TCPP (Figure 2a). The intensity of $_\mathrm{C-OH}$ in SZN-MOFs decreased distinctly, confirming the coordination between carboxyl groups and Cu ions (Figure 2a). Compared with MOFs using TCPP as ligands (termed as MOFs-TCPP), SZN-MOFs only had a single peak in the N 1  s XPS spectrum, further confirming the Zn-N coordination in SZN-MOFs (Figures S8 and S9, Supporting Information). Figure  2b exhibits the Zn K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra, where the absorption intensity of SZN-MOFs was close to Zn foil, but lower than that of $z\mathrm{nO}$ , suggesting that the Zn species in SZN-MOFs carried charge between $0{\rightarrow}2$ . Fourier-transformed (FT) $\mathrm{k}^{3}$ -weighted extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra further indicated that SZN-MOFs exhibited one main peak at ${\approx}1.52\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ， which was in accordance with Zn-N/O scattering path, while no $Z\mathrm{n-Zn}$ bond were observed, indicating that $Z\mathrm{n}$ species were atomic-level dispersed (Figure  2c). The EXAFS fitting spectroscopy of SZN-MOFs presented that the bond distance was ${\approx}2.01$ Å and the coordination number was ${\approx}6.0$ . The $\mathsf{N}/\mathsf{O}$ atoms adsorbing on partial Zn atoms perpendicular to the $\mathrm{{Zn-N_{4}}}$ plane in SZN-MOFs may result in an average coordination number of 6.0 (Figure  2d and Table S2, Supporting Information).[4f ]
## 2.2. POD-Like Activity Assays
The intrinsic POD-like activity of SZN-MOFs was measured by using colorimetric assays. The catalytic oxidation of $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) was used as a model reaction to study the catalytic activity of SZN-MOFs for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure 3a). As shown in Figure  3b and Figure S10 (Supporting Information), SZN-MOFs exhibited remarkable catalytic activity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , whereas, the POD-like catalytic performance of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -TCPP and MOFs-TCPP (2D MOF with TCPP as ligands) decline distinctly. The absorbance of oxidized TMB (oxTMB) catalyzed by SZN-MOFs could be ignored in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , suggesting their negligible oxidase-like activity (Figure  3b; Figure S10, Supporting Information). In addition, bulk SZN-MOFs exhibited lower POD-like activity than SZN-MOFs, which was attributed to their limited accessibility of active sites and large diffusion resistance (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Moreover, specific activity (SA) assays were further performed to quantify their POD-like activities.[16] SZN-MOFs exhibited better specific activity (SA, $6.34~\mathrm{U~mg^{-1})}$ 1 than $Z\mathrm{n}$ -TCPP $(0.75\mathrm{~U~mg^{-1}})$ and MOFs-TCPP $(0.63\mathrm{~U~mg^{-1}})$ , which further confirmed the remarkable POD-like activity of SZN-MOFs (Figure  3c). We concluded that the remarkable POD-like activity of SZN-MOFs was due to their unique structure. First, the abundant exposed atomically dispersed zinc sites in SZN-MOFs made them possess large amounts of catalytic active centers, which could considerably enhance their PODlike activity. Second, SZN-MOFs could significantly decrease the diffusion resistance in the catalytic reaction due to their ultrathin thickness.
The catalytic performance of SZN-MOFs significantly declined as increased KSCN concentrations, further demonstrating that the catalytic sites of SZN-MOFs were the atomically dispersed $Z\mathrm{n-N}_{4}$ sites (Figure 3d).[17] The SZN-MOFs possessed high catalytic stability under a series of $\mathrm{\pH}$ and temperature treatments (Figures S12 and S13, Supporting Information). In addition, the HAADF-STEM image showed that SZN-MOFs still possessed densely single zinc sites after incubating with LB broth, making SZN-MOFs potential applications in biomedicine (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Next, we investigated the effect of reaction temperature and pH on the catalytic performance of SZN-MOFs. The POD-like activity of SZN-MOFs reached a maximum at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then gradually declined as increased temperatures (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The SZN-MOFs exhibited the best catalytic performance at $\mathrm{pH}~3.6$ (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The optimum reaction conditions $\mathrm{(pH~}3.6\$ , $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ were similar to natural horseradish peroxidase (pH 4.5, $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ).[18]
To further evaluate the enzyme-like activity of SZN-MOFs, we measured the enzyme-reaction kinetics under wide concentration ranges of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and TMB. As shown in Figure  3e and Figure S17 (Supporting Information), the initial reaction rate increased linearly with the increasing substrate concentrations at low concentration ranges, but reached relatively constant at high substrate concentrations. The Michaelis–Menten constant $(K_{\mathrm{m}})$ was used to evaluate the affinity of substrates and SZNMOFs. A smaller $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value presents a higher affinity ability. Based on the Michaelis-Menten curves, the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ values of SZNMOFs were $132.35\mathrm{\mm}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $39~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for TMB, indicating good affinity ability toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and TMB (Figures S18 and S19, Supporting Information). In addition, the maximum rate $(V_{\mathrm{max}})$ of SZN-MOFs was determined to be $3.0\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~M~s}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $1.46\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~M~s^{-1}}$ for TMB (Figures S18 and S19, Supporting Information). The above reaction kinetics assays further confirmed that SZN-MOFs possessed significant PODlike catalytic activity.
The absorbance value of oxidized TMB distinctly decreased when adding additional isopropanol, confirming that the •OH was the active intermediate in the POD-like reactions of SZN-MOFs (Figure S20, Supporting Information).[4a] Then, we further demonstrated the generation of •OH by using fluorescent assays. An obvious •OH characteristic signal at $435\ \mathrm{nm}$ was observed in the presence of SZN-MOFs, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and TA (Figure S21, Supporting Information). In addition, electron spin resonance spectra (ESR) were also used to detect OH directly. There was a characteristic signal peak of 1:2:2:1 in SZN-MOFs $+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ group, indicating the production of •OH (Figure  2f). However, SZN-MOFs or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ alone could not generate •OH characteristic signal peaks. These results verified that SZN-MOFs as excellent POD-like nanozymes could catalyze decomposing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce •OH.
## 2.3. DFT Calculations
To explore the underlying mechanism responsible for the peroxidase-like activities, we performed DFT calculations for SZNMOFs. A cluster model was constructed to simulate the active center of SZN-MOFs, whose geometric structure was illustrated in Figure 4a. The proposed mechanism is shown in Figure 4b, where the SZN-MOFs is first oxidized to int1 by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and then the single oxygen atom on int1 abstracts reductant H from the substrate TMB generating int2 and the oxidated state of TMB (i.e., oxTMB). For notice, the calculated TMB is in its hydrogenated state and its oxidized state oxTMB is a cation radical that is colored in blue (Figure 4b). The int2 is five-coordinated and can be regarded as an active state compared to the stable six-coordinated conformation. Also, the geometry structure of int2 is similar to that of the iron porphyrin in the hemin during the catalysis process. Subsequently, int2 is oxidized by the second $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generating int3 that can continuously abstract two reductant H from two TMB and is converted to int4 and finally return to int2. In short, int2 acts as the catalyst to facilitate the oxidation of TMB by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . To verify the above mechanism, we calculated the corresponding Gibbs free energy profile along with the reaction coordinates. The results are presented in Figure  4c. The energy of the fifth stationary point containing int2 is calibrated as the energy reference point since the int2 is actually the catalyst. The results indicate that the first step, the oxidation of SZN with $_{0-0}$ bond cleavage, is the ratedetermining step (RDS) since it has the highest energy barrier of $37.2\mathrm{~kcal~mol}^{-1}$ . The oxidation of int2 has the second highest energy barrier of $23.7\mathrm{kcalmol^{-1}}$ , indicating that the reaction can occur spontaneously at room temperature. As a comparison, the energy barriers of the H abstraction reactions of int1, int3, and int4 are only 11.5, 16.7, and $13.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{\kcal\mol^{-1}}}$ , respectively, which depicts that these reactions are not RDS and are easy to occur. Although the first step has a relatively higher energy barrier of $37.2\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$ , the formation of int2 is accompanied by a significant energy release, which can push the reaction equilibrium toward the direction of int2 generation. Once formed, int2 can act as a catalyst to easily catalyze the oxidation of TMB by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with an RDS energy barrier of only $23.7\mathrm{kcalmol^{-1}}$ . In summary, the above results provide insights into the intrinsic peroxidase-like activity of SZN-MOFs and a possible underlying mechanism is verified.
## 2.4. In Vitro Antibiofilm Experiments
Given their remarkable POD-like activities, the SZN-MOFs can effectively catalyze the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate amounts of toxic •OH, which seriously damages the biofilm integrity and significantly promotes antibiofilm efficiency.[3c] The Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus) biofilm was used as a model to evaluate the in vitro antibiofilm activity. As shown in Figure 5a and Figure S22 (Supporting Information), the crystal violet (CV) stained biofilm after incubating with SZN-MOFs or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ alone exhibited similar deep purple with the control group. The color of CVstained biofilm was slightly lighter after incubating with ZnTCPP or MOFs-TCPP in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ because of their weak POD-like activity. However, the color of CV-stained biofilm under SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ co-treatment exhibited much lighter due to the efficient eradication of biofilm by the generated toxic •OH. The biofilm biomass by different treatments was quantified by a CV-stained colorimetric assay (Figure  5b).
In contrast, the biofilm biomass was significantly decreased after incubating with SZN-MOFs in the presence $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , further indicating that SZN-MOFs with excellent POD-like activity could remarkably enhance the in vitro antibiofilm efficiency.
The plate count experiment was conducted to evaluate the biofilm viability. There was no obvious change in the colony-forming units after incubating with HAc-NaAc buffer, SZN-MOFs, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , MOFs-TCPP $+\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Zn-TCPP+H_{2}O_{2}}$ respectively (Figure  5c,d). However, when incubating with SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the biofilm survival rate was $<10\%$ , which was mainly because of their generated amounts of toxic •OH (Figures 5c,d). In addition, SEM characterization was performed to observe the change of biofilm morphology. When incubating with SZNMOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ the biofilm cells were found to be seriously disrupted and became collapsed when compared with that of the other five groups (Figure 5e). These results were consistent with the plate count experiments, further confirming that SZN-MOFs possessed excellent antibiofilm activity at a low concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Table S3, Supporting Information).
To further evaluate the antibiofilm activity by SZN-MOFs, the biofilm thickness was measured by using a 3D confocal laser scanning microscopy (3D CLSM). The biofilm thickness after SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ treatment was ${\approx}2.8~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , which was much smaller than the other five groups, demonstrating the remarkable antibiofilm activity of SZN-MOFs under a low dosage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figures  5f; Figure S23, Supporting Information). Furthermore, live/dead biofilm assays were performed to study the antibiofilm efficiency by using SYTO 9/propidium iodide (PI) staining. Compared with the first three groups (i.e., control, SZN-MOFs, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ alone), the $\mathrm{Zn-TCPP+H_{2}O_{2}}$ and MOFs-TCPP + $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ treatments exhibited little red fluorescent signals due to their weak POD-like activity (Figure  5g). Notably, the number of dead cells was dramatically increased when incubating with SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , identified by large amounts of red fluorescence signals (Figure 5g). These results demonstrated that SZN-MOFs with enhanced POD-like activity exhibited dramatically in vitro antibiofilm activity because they could catalyze the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate toxic •OH.
## 2.5. In Vivo Antibiofilm Activity Evaluation
Encouraged by the prominent in vitro antibiofilm activity, we further evaluated the in vivo antibiofilm activity by SZN-MOFs. We first investigated the biocompatibility of SZN-MOFs. Cellular viability assay, pathological and hematological experiments, blood biochemical indicators, as well as hemolysis ratio experiments demonstrated that SZN-MOFs possessed excellent biocompatibility (Figures S24–S28, Supporting Information). The in vivo antibiofilm therapeutic process was shown in Figure 6a. Briefly, S. aureus biofilm $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $10^{8}\mathrm{CFU\mL^{-1}},$ was subcutaneously injected into the mice′s abdomen. The subcutaneous abscesses formed one day after infection. The infected mice were separately treated with HAc-NaAc buffer (control), SZN-MOFs, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{MOFs-TCPP}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{Zn-TCPP+H_{2}O_{2}}$ , and ${\mathrm{SZN}}{\mathrm{-MOFs}}+{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ once a day for seven days. Meanwhile, photographs were taken every two days. As shown in Figure 6b, the abscess area in wound was gradually decreased with the treatment time. The mice treated with SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ rapidly formed scabs and exhibited the best healing rate than that of the other five groups. The abscess treated with SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ had almost been healed after seven days, which was mainly because of the highly oxidative damage to wound biofilm infections induced by SZN-MOFs converting $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to toxic •OH. On the 7th day, the infected skins were cut and cultured for counting the colony-forming units by the plate count experiment. As shown in Figure 6c and Figure S29 (Supporting Information), when compared with the other five groups, the group treated by SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ showed few bacterial colonies. Meanwhile, no obvious body weight change was observed during therapeutic periods, suggesting the excellent biosafety of SZN-MOFs (Figure 6d). In addition, the histomorphological analysis was performed to evaluate the infected tissues on the 7th day. Hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) and Giemsa staining suggested that few inflammatory cells were observed in the group of SZN-MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , while there were large amounts of aggregated inflammatory cells in the other five groups (Figure 6e,f). These results confirmed that SZN-MOFs presented an excellent in vivo antibiofilm activity.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have reported a surfactant-assisted method for designing 2D MOFs-based single-site nanozymes, and used it for antibiofilm therapy. The 2D MOFs as supports were used to direct synthesis of the single zinc sites nanozyme, in which $Z\mathrm{n}$ atom loading is up to $4.6\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ . The as-synthesized SZNMOFs exhibited significant POD-like activity owing to densely Zn single-atom active sites and the ultrathin structure of 2D MOFs. ESR and fluorescent experiments verified the generation of •OH mediated by SZN-MOFs in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The generated •OH exhibited severely oxidative damage to biofilm cells due to their high oxidation potential. Thus, the SZNMOFs presented remarkable eradicating biofilm activity at a low dosage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which in vitro survival rate was $<10\%$ . In addition, in wound biofilm infected model, SZN-MOFs could significantly promote wound healing and showed high biocompatibility. This study provided a novel strategy for fabricating single-site nanozymes and exhibited great potential for biofilm infections application.
